  m_Name: AhabLoreSO
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  LoreTitle: Ahab
  Lore: "I. \u201CTo fear what you want is a terrible thing\u2026\u201D\r\n\r\nSome
    people were built for a life on the sea. At a young age, Ahab knew that his father
    was one of them and that it was his own fate to be connected to the ocean. Little
    Ahab, strolling along the beach where the water and sand met, never got close
    enough to even get his feet wet, but often looked out into the vast ocean in
    front of him, knowing that it stretched farther than he could ever imagine. He
    thought of the adventures that his father experienced on the oceans\u2014the
    ones that he had told him about before drifting off to sleep. As he looked out
    into the dark sea, the moon reflected upon its surface, he thought of pirates
    and sea monsters, but most of all, his father\u2019s encounter with a giant whale\u2014a
    creature that was the scale of something that he couldn\u2019t comprehend.\r\n\r\nIts
    smooth but wrinkled skin, its giant eye, and the echo of its call were things
    that he imagined so vividly, as if he had seen the creature himself. When he
    closed his eyes at night, the creature took up his entire field of vision, calling
    for him to come find it.\r\n\r#\n\u201COne day, I\u2019ll become a sailor and
    reach out to you.\u201D\r\n\r\nOn the day of his 13th birthday, Ahab had begged
    his father to take him out to sea.\r\n\r\n\u201CThe waters are dangerous, Ahab,\u201D
    his father warned, \u201Cespecially during this season.\u201D\r\n\r\nNaive and
    inexperienced, Ahab continued his pleading. The cold air and violent waves were
    not enough to cease his curiosity and his father was unwilling to disappoint
    his son on such an important day. Though Ahab may have gotten what he had wished
    for, the consequences were despairingly dreadful. From the day that he was 13
    years and 1 day old, when he closed his eyes at night, the creature took up Ahab\u2019s
    entire field of vision, taunting him\u2014just barely out of his reach.\r\n#\r\nII.
    \u201CYour face lingers but your warmth is gone\u2026\u201D\r\n\t\r\nThough Ahab
    feared the sea after his extreme encounter, he could not resist the allure of
    the waters. Wanting to be close to the ocean, but being too afraid to jump in,
    Ahab took apprenticeship under a group of fishmongers. Hours were long and his
    work was exhausting, but being next to the sea invigorated him and seeing the
    fresh catches of the day made it all worth his while. Still, the sight of sailors
    maneuvering back to land and docking their ships never failed to fill Ahab with
    a sense of regret, longing, and bitter jealousy.\r\n\r\nAhab, all grown up, was
    a fine fishmonger. His products were known to be of the highest quality and they
    were always prepared with the finest touch. A group of experienced sailors wished
    to do business with him, to which Ahab agreed. This deal was a deal of a lifetime,
    but Ahab had other motives for accepting such a deal\u2014the daughter of one
    of the sailors, training to become a sailor herself. \n# \nEach day that their
    ship pulled into shore, Ahab was eager to hear her stories\u2014the ones about
    pirates, and sea monsters, and most of all, her encounter with creatures whose
    size he could not comprehend. The two sat on the pier, the moonlight reflected
    upon her excitement-filled eyes as she told him of her adventures. Each time,
    they sat a little closer and grew a little fonder of one another.\r\n\r\nAfter
    years of heart-pounding conversations, the sailor\u2019s daughter, Cordelia,
    became smitten with Ahab\u2019s curiosity and gentleness\u2014a direct contrast
    to his rugged and intimidating appearance\u2014while Ahab fell madly in love
    with Cordelia\u2019s audacious spirit and rough hands that served as evidence
    of her years at sea\u2014a direct contrast to her soft face. The two fell in
    love and had a son, Ishmael, who was the perfect mix of the two\u2014a delicate
    appearance with an inextinguishable curiosity.\r\n\r\nOnly when things are going
    well do the biggest disasters come as a surprise. \r\n\r#\nOn the day of his
    13th birthday, Ishmael had begged his mother to take him out to sea. He had dreamed
    of following in his mother\u2019s footsteps, daring to conquer the great ocean
    and see with his own eyes the beast that shook his father to his core. He would
    have never expected that his mother, scouting the route for their voyage, would
    succumb to the strength of the waters.\r\n\r\nIshmael\u2019s delicate features
    reminded Ahab, everyday, of Cordelia\u2019s presence.\r\n\r\n\u201CYou are the
    most important thing to me, Ishmael,\u201D Ahab repeated everyday, \u201Cso do
    not go after that horrid beast.\u201D\r\n\r#\nIII. \u201CDesperation and longing
    drives us to extremes\u2026\u201D\r\n\r\nA decade after the loss of his wife,
    though Ahab had learned to live without her, he may never have truly recovered
    from his grief. His love and curiosity for the ocean was replaced by a thick
    disgust that penetrated every aspect of his life. How could something so calming
    be so destructive? How could something so precious to someone be taken away with
    such absence of thought?\r\n\r\nThough Ahab continued running his business as
    usual, the feeling of loss was palpable to everyone he interacted with. Most
    simply felt pity for him, but for his son, Ahab\u2019s stark change in behavior
    created a heavy guilt in Ishmael. \r\n\r\nToo foolish to heed his father's warning,
    Ishmael continued to chase after the sight of a whale. Surely, if he could conquer
    the beast and show his father his colossal catch, he would spring back to his
    old self. The sight would be too great to ignore. So, like his mother, Ishmael
    trained to be a strong sailor\u2014one that could weather any storm.\r\n\r#\nHowever,
    after years and years of sailing, Ishmael failed to capture the creature despite
    having the opportunity to do so many times. He could not predict the patterns
    that whales followed and always lost sight of them as soon as he caught a glimpse.
    Rumors of a potion seller that sold particularly potent concoctions caught his
    ear.\r\n\r\nTempted by the promise of success, Ishmael took an offer. He traded
    lungs for gills and legs for fins\u2014to know the patterns of a beast, one must
    put themselves into the shoes of one.\r\n\r#\nIV. \u201CFear can be overcome
    but the consequences are dire\u2026\u201D\r\n\r\nThe loss of his father and wife
    destroyed Ahab, but the disappearance of his son racked his mind. For years,
    Ahab continued to wonder what had become of his son. On the day that he disappeared,
    Ishmael's ship docked safely and his shipmates were well and good. It seemed
    as though the expedition was a normal one aside from the unexplained disappearance
    of Ishmael.\r\n\r\nYears later, Ahab caught wind of the potion seller that had
    transformed Ishamel into a creature that he detested. \r\n\r\n\"There is no cure.
    No reversal,\" the seller insisted. Still, Ahab would try.\r\n\r#\nRegardless
    of the effort that Ahab might have spent, exhausting himself to get back the
    biggest reminder of Cordelia that he had left, it would be no use. \r\n\r\n\"Ahab!\"
    a fellow fishmonger ran up in a panic, \"Your son! The fishermen say they got
    your son!\"\r\n\r\nIn a frenzy, Ahab ran to the pier where he found a hunter,
    flopped over catch in hand as though it had never been anything but a husk to
    begin with. Though there was never a way for Ahab to truly confirm that that
    was in fact his son, the shark's dead eyes looked awfully familiar. The sea had
    taken everything away from him, so it would be foolish to believe that it would
    stop here.\r\n\r\nOvercome with rage, Ahab ignored the boundary between sea and
    land, lunging at the hunter with abandon. He fought with everything he had, but
    he was unaccustomed to the water as a result of avoiding it his whole life. But
    what else could he do but try?\r\n\r#\nV.  \u201CCan a life be given for a life?\u201D\r\n\r\nEmerging
    from the water, Ahab sputtered. The waters were unlike the ocean that he had
    stepped into. It was clean, almost rocking him to sleep before he violently fought
    his way up. Seeing a dark figure, without knowing why or how, Ahab knew that
    he would be given a second chance at life.\r\n\r\n\"Please, let my son live instead.
    I have lived a life of misery and don't wish to return. But my son\u2026he is
    young and has a sense of adventure that would be put to good use. Please.\"\r\n\r\n\"...Fine.
    But, he must fight alongside you.\"\r\n"
